### 5. Gap Analysis

> **GAP [CRITICAL]:** The "70-80% of value left on the table" claim is unattributed. — Marcus says it's "based on research across hundreds of AI deployments" and that "studies consistently show" the 20-30% figure for operational efficiency. No source is named. — The article's central thesis rests on this number. If it can't be sourced or attributed, the piece's credibility collapses at the first citation check by a skeptical CTO.

> **GAP [CRITICAL]:** The $2M CFO war story has no resolution. — Marcus describes the conversation he has with CFOs who can't see the ROI, but never states whether the reframe works, whether programs get saved, or what happens when they don't. — Without an outcome, this is advice, not a story. The article loses its most emotionally resonant section.

> **GAP [CRITICAL]:** "Organizational intelligence" is introduced as a value dimension but never defined or illustrated. — Marcus names three dimensions of AI value (operational efficiency, strategic differentiation, organizational intelligence) but only explains the first clearly. "Organizational intelligence" gets one sentence: AI understanding patterns in your workforce. — If this is a genuine third pillar of the value framework, it needs at least one concrete example. Without it, the framework feels incomplete and readers may dismiss it as filler.

> **GAP [NICE-TO-HAVE]:** The "three times more likely to scale" stat needs a source. — Marcus states that companies with a structured AI value framework are about three times more likely to successfully scale beyond pilot. This is a strong claim and highly quotable — but unattributed. — Attribution would convert this from anecdote to evidence. Without it, a skeptical reader discounts it.

> **GAP [NICE-TO-HAVE]:** The data readiness war story has no setup or outcome. — Marcus says he's seen organizations spend millions on AI infrastructure only to discover their data quality is too poor. No sector, no scale, no what-happened-next. — A single concrete detail would make this a usable story rather than a cautionary maxim.

> **GAP [NICE-TO-HAVE]:** The 2-3 year urgency window is asserted without reasoning. — Marcus says organizations that wait another two or three years will face a structural disadvantage. What specifically closes the window? Is it talent concentration at early movers, compounding data advantages, customer expectation shifts? — Without the mechanism, this reads as urgency-for-urgency's-sake, which sophisticated readers will discount.

> **GAP [NICE-TO-HAVE]:** The 2.5-4x ROI over three years figure is unattributed and context-free. — Is this across all industries? All company sizes? All AI program types? What's the variance? — If this number could be narrowed to "consulting-sector firms that completed foundational data readiness work" it becomes far more defensible.

